{#- ### Verdict — renders opening posture statement, a lead-in line that
    introduces the worst-case blockquote, the red HTML blockquote itself,
    and a closing call-to-action.

    M3.14 — the `bullets_intro` field frames the blockquote so the reader
    knows what they are about to scan. Without it the bullets render as a
    bare list dropped after the opening — visually disconnected from the
    verdict prose. Backward-compat: when the fragment doesn't carry
    `bullets_intro`, a sensible default is emitted.

    Inputs: data.severity, data.opening, data.bullets_intro (optional),
             data.bullets[{title,body,refs}], data.closing.
             severity_emoji is a filter-injected helper.
             verified_suffixes[] (renderer-computed, one per bullet): a
             plain-language ` — ✓ verified attack path` badge for bullets whose
             findings anchor a fully-viable abuse chain, else '' (abuse-cases.json).
             Finding and design-risk references render as compact evidence links.
             business_context_note (renderer-computed, optional): declared no-harm scope.
             method_limits (renderer-computed, unconditional): one sentence on
             the method and any screened or unanalysed components.
-#}
### Verdict

{{ severity_emoji(data.severity) }} {{ data.opening }}
{% if risk_distribution %}
{{ risk_distribution }}
{% endif %}{% if business_context_note %}
{{ business_context_note }}
{% endif %}
{% if method_limits %}
{{ method_limits }}
{% endif %}
<br/>

**{{ data.bullets_intro or ("Residual risks worth reviewing:" if data.severity == "green" else "Security concerns behind this assessment:") }}**

<blockquote style="border-left: 3px solid #dc2626; background: #fef2f2; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 0;">

{% for bullet in data.bullets -%}
- **{{ bullet.title }}** — {{ bullet.body }}{{ verified_suffixes[loop.index0] if verified_suffixes else "" }}
{% endfor %}
</blockquote>

<br/>

{{ data.closing }}
